@* The band every page opens with: an eyebrow saying which part of the app this is, the page's
   name, a line of what it holds, and whatever the page's own actions are on the right.

   Eleven pages were building this by hand and it had come apart in small ways — Settings had lost
   its eyebrow entirely (and with it the `mt-2` that only exists because there is one above), the
   back link above the eyebrow was written twice with the icon a different size each time, and the
   heading's own classes were retyped at every call site. The shape is the same everywhere, so it
   is one component and the differences are parameters.

   Back / TitleSuffix / Actions cover the three places a page has ever wanted to add something:
   above the eyebrow, beside the heading, and at the right-hand end of the band. *@

<div class="page-head">
    <div class="min-w-0">
        @if (BackHref is not null)
        {
            <a href="@BackHref" title="@BackTitle"
               class="mb-2 flex w-fit items-center gap-1 text-sm text-ink-500 transition-colors hover:text-ink-700">
                <Icon Name="arrow-left" Class="w-3.5 h-3.5"/> @BackLabel
            </a>
        }
        <span class="eyebrow">@Eyebrow</span>
        @if (TitleSuffix is null)
        {
            <h1 class="page-title mt-2 @TitleClass">@Title</h1>
        }
        else
        {
            @* The badge shares the heading's baseline rather than sitting under it, so a game's
               name and its outcome read as one line. *@
            <div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1.5">
                <h1 class="page-title @TitleClass">@Title</h1>
                @TitleSuffix
            </div>
        }
        @if (Sub is not null)
        {
            <p class="page-sub">@Sub</p>
        }
    </div>
    @if (Actions is not null)
    {
        @* One row, whatever the page puts in it: a search box beside a button, a dropdown, a pair
           of buttons. Two pages had wrapped their actions in this same row by hand and the rest
           had not, which is how a third action can end up a different distance from its neighbour
           than on the page beside it. *@
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 @ActionsClass">@Actions</div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Which part of the app this is — "Match history", "Reference", "Your table".</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Eyebrow { get; set; } = "";

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>The line under the heading: what the page holds, in a phrase. Omit where the
    /// heading says all there is to say.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Sub { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Extra classes on the heading — in practice only <c>italic</c>, for a game with
    /// no name of its own.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? TitleClass { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Sits on the heading's baseline — a game's status badge.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? TitleSuffix { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The page's own actions, at the right-hand end of the band — laid out as one row.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Actions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Extra classes on the actions row — the games list runs it full width on a phone,
    /// so the search box in it can be.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? ActionsClass { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Where the way back goes, for a page opened out of a list. Omit for a page that
    /// is reached from the sidebar and has nothing to go back to.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? BackHref { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string BackLabel { get; set; } = "Back";

    /// <summary>The link's tooltip, where "back" needs saying more precisely — the game page
    /// returns to the filtered slice it was opened from, not to the whole history.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? BackTitle { get; set; }
}
